NAME
       kill - send a signal to processes

SYNOPSIS
       kill [-s sig | -SIG | -n num] target ...
       kill -l [sig]

DESCRIPTION
       Sends a signal to each TARGET. The default is TERM. A target is a
       pid, -PGID for every process in that process group, or a process
       name, which reaches every process of that name you may signal.
       In csh, %N stands for the pid of job N and %% for the latest job.

       A user may signal only their own processes; root may signal any.
       Before signalling init or a kernel thread, kill asks for
       confirmation.

OPTIONS
       -l       list the signal names; with an argument, translate a
                number to a name or a name to a number
       -s SIG   send SIG, given by name or number
       -SIG     the same: -9, -KILL, -kill or -SIGKILL
       -n NUM   send the signal with number NUM

EXAMPLES
       kill 42
       kill -9 doom
       kill -s INT 42
       kill -TERM -- -42
       kill %1

SEE ALSO
       killall(1), ps(1), sysmon(1), signals in sigtest(1)
